  ]
};

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What does a lab container in Berlin cost?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The investment depends on biosafety level, cleanroom class, foundation type and site location. An analytics container in Adlershof sits in a different order of magnitude from a BSL-3 container with a HEPA H14 airlock for a university hospital or an infection-research institute. We calculate project by project — including pile foundations where the Spree water table is high, vibration isolation at the Adlershof research campus, and fire protection under the Berlin Hospital Building Ordinance (KhBauVO Berlin) on hospital grounds. Send us your site data and we will respond within 24 hours with an indicative range."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Which Berlin borough office is competent for my container laboratory?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Berlin has no central building-control authority — each of the 12 borough offices (Bezirksämter) runs its own building control with its own practice. Mitte reviews differently from Treptow-Köpenick (Adlershof) or Pankow (Buch). The Berlin Building Code (Bauordnung Berlin, BauO Bln) always applies. We identify the competent borough office from your address and submit review-ready building documents matched to that borough’s practice — saving weeks compared with a standard application that has to be revised three times."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How long does delivery of a lab container from Albstadt to Berlin take?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The distance is around 720 km — by heavy-goods transport north on the A81, east on the A6, north on the A9 via Würzburg and Nuremberg to the Berlin Ring (A10) and the Berlin-Schönefeld, Berlin-Süd or Berlin-Spandau junctions. Pure driving time is about 7.5 hours; with the required permits and escort vehicles it is typically one transport day. From order to operational handover in Berlin takes 8 to 14 weeks."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Will a standard container withstand Berlin’s sandy soil and Spree groundwater?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Berlin lies in the Berlin–Warsaw glacial valley on sandy soil of soil class 3 to DIN 18196. Inner-city groundwater often sits 2 to 4 m below ground level — and periodically higher near the Spree. Standard pad foundations settle visibly on such ground after 18 to 36 months. We calculate pile or raft foundations to DIN 4017/4020 with hydraulic uplift protection on a site-specific basis — instruments keep their calibration, and cleanroom classes keep their certifiability."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can Planexus install a lab container on a hospital or research campus in Berlin?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes, that is technically possible. Siting on a hospital campus in Mitte or at the Berlin-Buch campus would require coordination with the relevant hospital building office and Berlin’s university building administration. Fire protection under the Berlin Hospital Building Ordinance (KhBauVO Berlin), sterile delivery along designated logistics axes and coordination with the fire directorate are standard. On hospital and research campuses in Berlin-Buch or other Berlin sites, crane-based, campus-compliant installation is possible — without disrupting ongoing hospital operations."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What requirements apply to BSL-3 containers in Berlin?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "BSL-3 in Berlin requires a permit under the German Biological Agents Ordinance (BioStoffV) — federal law — a notification to LAGeSo (Berlin’s state office for health and social affairs) and, where applicable, the competent borough public-health office. For genetic-engineering facilities S1/S2/S3 an additional notification under the German Genetic Engineering Act (GenTG) is required. Technically the ordinance requires a negative-pressure cascade of at least −50 Pa, HEPA H14 on the extract-air path, autoclaved airlocks and redundant ventilation. We supply the risk assessment, safety concept and complete application documents — containers are tested to TRBA 100 and handed over with IQ/OQ/PQ."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Hire or buy — what makes sense for Berlin pharma or research projects?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Rule of thumb: for project durations under 24 months — for example interim laboratories during hospital refurbishments, time-limited third-party-funded projects or short-term pharma study campaigns — hire pays. For a permanent expansion at the Adlershof research campus, in the Berlin-Brandenburg pharma cluster or at the Berlin-Buch campus, purchase usually prevails, often with a hire-purchase option. We compare both models for your Berlin project, including balance-sheet impact and residual value."
      }
    }
  ]
};
